    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The tactile minimalism of the season is equally defined by an evolution in silhouette, where structured shoulders and belted waists now act as anchors for heavy, draping fabrics. Collaborations between Lagos House and brands like Saint Laurent are showcasing how oversized blazers can redirect the weight of knitted tunics downward, creating a silhouette that feels grounding yet fluid. This architectural approach sees Louis Vuitton and Hermès operating with overlapping layers that balance volume without appearing bulky, utilizing the inherent drape of their materials to create a new kind of tailored ease.\n\nFashion alternates between fluid, A-line cuts and rigid, boxy trousers that mimic the preppy energy often seen at Gucci or Balenciaga, proving that sophistication now lies in the manipulation of volume. As seen in recent looks from Chanel and Loewe, the interplay between sharp tailoring and soft, messy finishes defines the modern silhouette, allowing heavy fabrics to move naturally without restriction. By leveraging the distinct aesthetic languages of Dior and Prada, the season demonstrates that the most powerful silhouette is one that feels engineered for comfort while maintaining an unmistakably high-fashion presence.",
      "source": "target",
